{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgSB8HXyJqD2GNdgPZ7TH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the generator: the lower bound of each heart-rate target is now sent as `start` instead of `value`. It's still sent as a percentage of LTHR (`%lthr`), so Intervals still turns it into bpm using its own LTHR of 172. I haven't checked it on the watch, because I didn't push anything to Intervals.\n\n**Why the watch clamped the ranges:** the last event we sent gave each target as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. With `value`, Intervals sends the target to the watch as a zone target, and the watch snaps it to its own zone bands. With `start`, it goes over as a fixed bpm range that the watch shows as given. That fits `readback/watch-notes.md`: every range the watch showed was exactly one of its zone bands, and changing the watch's LTHR moved the bands but never gave the planned numbers. The fix is one line in `hr_target()` in `gen.py`. I regenerated `plans/tempo-run.json` and every heart-rate step now has `start`/`end`, with no `value` left.\n\n**To confirm:** re-push with `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json > event.json && ./push.sh event.json`, sync the watch, and check that it shows 117–129, 151–160, 129–141 and 112–124. Those are the plan × 172. The watch's own LTHR is 168, so seeing these exact numbers proves the range comes from Intervals and not the watch.\n\n**Colleague's note:**\n- **Used:** the `start`-not-`value` finding (that's the whole fix), keeping percentages rather than absolute bpm (our notes show the same two failed attempts they described), and the 172-vs-168 check.\n- **Not used:** the advice on the top-level `description` text. Nobody reported missing steps in the web UI, and the readback shows the steps were accepted. A badly formatted description can make Intervals wipe the steps entirely, so adding one untested was a risk with no problem to solve.\n- **Not done:** creating test events and syncing the watch, because of the rule against touching external services.\n\nI haven't committed anything. `ANSWER.md` has the full explanation."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1431,"cache_read_input_tokens":24868,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1431},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8ad82232-f61c-4eef-ae11-c8b838defdc7","uuid":"826d13ec-1915-4dc4-a94d-82dcbf3f9e20","timestamp":"2026-10-27T05:17:38.664Z","request_id":"req_011CgSB8H3CpbABt2SyTguU6"}
